|re2c| can accelerate hot greedy loops in regular expressions using SIMD
hardware vectorization via declarative syntax templates (Go 1.27 ``simd/archsimd``
and C AVX2 intrinsics).

In lexical analysis, a significant percentage of CPU cycles is spent
consuming long homogeneous byte sequences (such as string literals,
identifiers, or whitespace).

Instead of processing these loops byte-by-byte with scalar branch dispatches,
the vectorization engine emits a fast-forward loop that loads and evaluates
blocks of 32 bytes simultaneously using parallel range checks and bitmask extraction.

When enabled with ``--vectorize-loops`` or ``re2c:vectorize:loops = 1;``:

- Vectorization applies only to normal code generation with the goto/label code
  model and single-byte code units. It is disabled with ``--eager-skip``, storable
  state, or an end-of-input rule (``re2c:eof``).
- The compiler selects base states with a nonzero refill requirement and a
  reflexive character class of at most 8 disjoint ranges. The loop must not record
  backtracking positions, perform tag actions or have hoisted cursor increments
  or end-of-input transitions. Tags must be absent from the entire DFA, not just
  from the loop.
- Both ``re2c:yyfill:enable`` and ``re2c:yyfill:check`` must be enabled to ensure
  the vector loop stops before the end-of-buffer boundary. It reserves enough
  lookahead for the scalar dispatcher and does not increase ``YYMAXFILL``.
- The generic API is not supported, because the vector loop reads input directly
  through ``YYCURSOR`` rather than via ``YYPEEK`` and ``YYSKIP``; use the simple or
  record API.
- States that do not meet these requirements retain scalar code without a
  diagnostic. Enabling the option for a backend without the ``vectorize-loops``
  feature is an error, rather than a silent fallback.
- Loop code is emitted through the declarative language syntax template ``code:vector_loop``,
  supporting Go 1.27 (``simd/archsimd``) and C (AVX2), while remaining extensible to other
  targets without compiler modification.
- Once a delimiter byte outside the reflexive class is detected or buffer bounds are approached,
  execution falls back seamlessly to the scalar DFA dispatcher.
- The default templates test the complement of the class: every code unit that leaves the
  loop is compared for equality (or checked with an unsigned range test for a range of such
  code units), and the cursor is advanced to the first of them before the scalar dispatcher
  takes over. In Go the generated code uses ``bits.TrailingZeros32``, so the file must import
  ``math/bits`` in addition to ``simd/archsimd``. In C the generated code uses AVX2 intrinsics
  from ``<immintrin.h>`` (the file must include it and be compiled with AVX2 enabled, e.g.
  ``-mavx2``) and ``__builtin_ctz``, which requires GCC or Clang; for other compilers
  ``code:vector_loop`` can be redefined in a custom syntax file.
- The default C vector template requires C99 or later (or C++): it mixes
  declarations and statements inside a block. Unlike scalar output, this
  template is not compatible with C89.
- Both default templates require AVX2 support at run time. The generated lexer
  does not detect CPU capabilities or select a scalar implementation on CPUs
  without AVX2; applications that need this must check support before calling
  the vectorized lexer. Go requires ``GOEXPERIMENT=simd`` when building.
- The default C and Go templates enter the vector block only if the current code unit is in the
  class (a scalar test against the complement, placed after the length guard). Runs that end right
  away, such as one-character identifiers or empty string literals, are thus left to the scalar
  dispatcher and do not pay for the vector block. The test only selects the path: the vector
  block itself finds the exact exit position, so the result does not depend on it. Neither form
  guarantees a speedup for short runs or near the end of the buffer.
- The default C template explicitly calls ``_mm256_zeroupper()`` after the vector
  loop, and the Go template calls ``archsimd.ClearAVXUpperBits()``. These clear
  the upper AVX register bits before returning to scalar code, avoiding penalties
  when subsequent code uses legacy SSE instructions. C compiler insertion of
  ``vzeroupper`` is not reliable at all optimization levels, so the C template
  does not rely on it.
- The Go cleanup covers normal exits from the vector loop, not compiler-inserted
  ``runtime.panicBounds`` paths for invalid input bounds. The caller must maintain
  ``0 <= YYCURSOR <= YYLIMIT <= len(YYINPUT)`` and a correct length guard. Under
  these conditions the vector load's bounds checks cannot fail. If the bounds
  or a custom guard violate this contract, a panic may bypass
  ``ClearAVXUpperBits``; AVX cleanup during panic unwinding is not guaranteed.
- Both default templates write the length guard with ``lessthan``, which is rendered from
  ``code:yylessthan`` for the simple or record API, so a custom syntax definition
  of the length check applies to the vector loop as well as to the scalar states.
  This does not enable vectorization with the generic API.


The ``code:vector_loop`` template interface is documented in the syntax files
section. It provides both the character class and its complement as lists of
inclusive ranges. Testing the complement can be cheaper for classes such as
string bodies, where a few exit code units are compared for equality and the
first match gives the exact exit position.

Go string input
---------------

The default Go vector template requires ``YYINPUT`` to be a byte slice:
``archsimd.LoadUint8x32`` does not accept a ``string``. For string input, use
the existing ``re2c:YYINPUT`` configuration to name a read-only byte view.
Construct this view once, before entering the generated lexer, using the
documented ``unsafe.StringData`` and ``unsafe.Slice`` functions, and import
``unsafe`` in addition to ``math/bits`` and ``simd/archsimd``. For example:

.. code-block:: go

    func lex(text string) int {
        input := unsafe.Slice(unsafe.StringData(text), len(text))
        yycursor, yylimit := 0, len(input)
        /*!re2c
            re2c:api = simple;
            re2c:YYCTYPE = byte;
            re2c:YYINPUT = input;
            re2c:YYFILL = "return -2";
            re2c:YYFILL:naked = 1;
            re2c:vectorize:loops = 1;
            [a-z]+ { return yycursor }
            *      { return -1 }
        */
    }

The view aliases the string's storage without copying it. Neither generated
code nor user actions or refill code may modify its bytes. The slice retains
a pointer to the underlying storage, keeping it live while the lexer reads
it; no conversion through ``uintptr`` or separate ``runtime.KeepAlive`` call
is needed. Its length must not exceed ``len(text)``. For an empty string,
``StringData`` may return nil, and ``Slice`` with length zero is valid; the
normal length guard prevents any load. If refill code replaces the string,
it must rebuild the view and update the cursor and limit consistently before
resuming the lexer. The usual input bounds contract still applies.

Do not put ``[]byte(text[...])`` in the vector loop: Go may copy every block
and call the runtime with dirty AVX registers. Converting all inputs through
``unsafe.StringData(string(input))`` is not an equivalent general solution:
for byte slices the conversion to string may itself copy and call the runtime.
Byte-slice lexers should continue to use their original input directly.

The Go template retains five conditional cursor increments with untyped
constants to support both ``int`` and ``uint`` cursors. Their cost relative
to a single typed addition depends on the distribution of exit positions;
neither form is uniformly faster. No replacement has
been validated by a controlled performance comparison, so no performance
improvement is claimed for cursor advancement.
